00:02:42,220 --> 00:03:06,220
It was our first night out. camping Went all through the troop, Everyone was stirring. What a loud, happy group.

24
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:13,580
The tents were all pitched and the fire was lit. as the Scouts sang their songs and each patrol did a skit.

25
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:20,260
Soon they were nestled all snug in their beds as visions of instant oatmeal danced in their heads.

26
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:28,660
The Scouter's and Neckerchief's silence and ploughing had settled ourselves for a long winter's snoring.

27
00:03:37,140 --> 00:03:44,220
When out on the campsite there arose such a clatter, I unzipped my sleeping bag to see what was the matter.

28
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:51,579
I crawled to the door and put on my cap, unzipped the tent and looked out. the flap,

29
00:03:51,579 --> 00:03:57,500
The moon on the carpet of leaves on the ground gave a luster of midday, as I looked around,

30
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:06,780
When what then appeared to my initial survey was eight tiny raccoons and a miniature sled

31
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:13,980
With a big hulking driver, all covered in hair, I knew in a moment it must be there,

32
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:30,380
Silent and stealthy, his raccoons. they came as he growled and grunted and called them by a name:

33
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:37,980
Now Nasher, now Chewer, now Masher and Eater, on Gordra, on Nibbler, on Scarfer and Feter,

34
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:43,660
to the picnic table in the cooler, with no time of waste, and the food these Scouts brought

35
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:49,500
is ours for the tasting, as leaves that, before the wild hurricane, fly, when they meet with an

36
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:56,540
obstacle, mount up to the sky, so up to the coolers, his raccoons. they flew and filled their sleigh

37
00:04:56,540 --> 00:05:03,500
and their fat bellies too. I thrust out my head and I made a loud sound. Everything stopped

38
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:05,820
and the bear turned around.

39
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:18,460
He was covered in fur, from his head to his paw and his coat shown in the moonlight as

40
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:26,300
he opened his maw, his eyes, how they glowed, his teeth how keen, and the claws on his paws

41
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:33,659
were the longest I've seen. Half a bagel was tight clenched in his teeth and the package encircled

42
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:40,060
his head like a wreath. He had a broad face and a giant round belly that shook when he growled

43
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:47,580
like a bowl full of jelly. He gazed straight at me and he said: look here, we'll leave you plenty.

44
00:05:47,580 --> 00:05:53,580
but one night a year we get a few goodies for our cubs and kits. We'll leave the ramen noodles

45
00:05:53,580 --> 00:06:05,180
and other sundry vets. A wink of his eye and a twist of his head,

46
00:06:05,820 --> 00:06:12,780
Sud, gave me to know I had nothing to dread. He sprang to his sleigh to his team, gave a growl

47
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:19,500
and away. they all flew stealthy as a barn owl. But I heard him exclaim ere he flew into the woods:

48
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:24,140
be prepared next time, scouts, and thanks for the goods.

